Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:45:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 114/119] fix: the wave glare's log line said "half-res" about
 the deposit layer and was read as the source, and the report now says what a
 ray is actually worth

TEST-CHECKLIST K1/K4: a night lamp at Streak Amount 50 shows no rays and needs 70,
and r_HDRDiffractionDebug 3 was read as reporting the half-res copy as the threshold
source - i.e. that round 3's full-resolution read (b372dde2) was not active.

It was active. The proof is in the user's own other two results. The gate
IsSceneReferredStage(5) is read by CHDRStreaks::GetParams() at the top of
ExecuteStreaks() and by the source selection a few lines into ExecuteDiffraction(),
which ExecuteStreaks() calls - the same thread, the same call stack, and a gate that
LatchSceneReferredExposure() sets once per render view before pipeline->Execute().
K2 PASSED (ten units of Streak Amount = one stop), and that mapping exists only
inside the same if (IsSceneReferredStage(5)) in GetParams(), so stage 5 was on. Rays
APPEARED at 70, and ExecuteDiffractionComposite() returns early unless $HDRTarget has
a device texture, so the target existed. Stage 5 true and $HDRTarget present is
exactly the condition for pGlareSrc = pFullHDR. There is no third branch.

What K4 read was "own half-res layer" - the glare's own DEPOSIT target, half
resolution since 5587483d - nineteen words before "threshold source =" in the same
240-character sentence. A diagnostic that can be misread will be, and three days went
into a branch that was being taken. The source is now its own line, printed first, in
capitals, with the source's pixel dimensions and the reason, and the words "half-res"
appear in it only when the half-res copy really was what was read. The deposit layer
is called "own deposit layer". A scene-referred frame that somehow reaches the
selection with no $HDRTarget now says so once in CryLogAlways instead of silently
costing three and a half stops.

Round 3 recovered four stops (110 -> 70) rather than fourteen, and that is consistent:
the trace costed the WORST case, a lamp one native pixel across where the firefly
filter's 1-of-4 block gives x30 000. A lamp big enough to fill blocks round-trips that
filter exactly, so neither fix had fifteen stops to give back. The mechanism was
right; the case was not the one on screen.

The two stops that remain cannot be argued, only measured, because the unknown is the
lamp. r_HDRDiffractionDebug 3 now prints, once a second, in the units the camera's own
controls are in: the source peak in exposed units and in stops over mid grey and where
it is; the threshold both ways; the fraction of a source's light the pupil scatters
into the pattern; what the sprite's inner fade keeps of it; the resolved gain and the
Streak Amount it came from; and the line the mode exists for - what a SPIKE lands at a
stated number of native pixels out, in stops versus mid grey, with a verdict and the
Streak Amount that would put it on mid grey. Past R_tex the whole chain is
deposit(r) = excess * gain * I_ring(theta) * R_tex^2 / r^2, in which the texel pitch
cancels exactly, so the report is arithmetic and not an interpolation.

Two errors in the reasoning of rounds 2 and 3, both now instrumented rather than
argued:

  * The ring's MEAN is not the ray. m_fTailCoefficient carries the boundary ring's
    angular average - right for an energy budget, wrong for brightness, because a
    bladed iris runs 30-300x between a spike and the gap beside it. Every estimate
    those rounds made was made off the dark part of the pattern. GetRingPeak() now
    exists and the kernel's log prints the ray/gap contrast, the core radius and R_tex.
  * The sprite's inner fade is not "a small extra deficit". The kernel texel pitch is
    lambda*N*d/L, about 0.047 half-res px at f/8 on a 1080p frame, so the whole kernel
    TEXTURE spans some twelve half-res pixels and w_in = smoothstep(0, 4, r) covers the
    first four of them. Energy grows as ln(u) outside the core, so the fade keeps
    roughly a third of the texture's share: one and a half stops that the logged
    "deposited glare fraction" has never included. It does NOT dim the rays, which live
    past r = 4 px in the 1/u^2 tail, so it is not narrowed here - that would not answer
    the evidence and would re-open the blocks artefact three rounds were spent closing.
    It becomes the named constant kDiffractionInnerWindowHalfPx, its cost is printed,
    and narrowing it is written up as approach B for the next round.

Audited and clean, recorded so the next round does not re-walk it: the 5-tap pre-blur's
weights sum to exactly 1; the kernel mip chain box-filters LINEAR intensity with the
sqrt applied afterwards, so k*k really is the footprint mean; the half-res deposit
layer's additive upscale preserves radiance, which is the correct semantics; the six
spectral copies are renormalised to white in the working space.

No shader is touched and no constant moves - the picture is bit-identical on both
paths. decisions/s8-glare-source.md round 4 has the full argument and the three
approaches; DiffractionKernelSpec.md 4.2 is corrected on the inner window and on
reading the far field off the ring's mean.

